::: {.doc-subject}

Add an Erdős-Rényi G(n,m) random graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L11519-L11655){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_gnm_graph(
    n,
    m,
    directed=None,
    seed=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![G(n, m) random graph](../assets/graphs/add-gnm-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three random graphs on the same ring of eight nodes with exactly m=4, 8, and 12 edges. The edge count is fixed by m while which pairs get joined is random." width="100%"}

Creates a graph with `n` nodes and exactly `m` randomly placed edges. This model is useful
for generating random graphs with a fixed number of edges.

The method adds `n` new, unlabelled nodes, lists every possible edge among them, shuffles
that list, and keeps the first `m`. Every set of `m` distinct edges is equally likely. In
an undirected graph the candidates are the `n(n - 1) / 2` unordered pairs; in a directed
graph they are the `n(n - 1)` ordered pairs, so `u -> v` and `v -> u` are separate
candidates. Self-loops and duplicate edges are never created. Existing nodes are left
untouched and never connected to the new ones.

G(n, m) is the fixed-size counterpart of `add_gnp_graph()`. Use it when the exact edge
count matters, for example:

- building a random null model with the same number of nodes and edges as an observed
  network
- benchmarking algorithms at a precise size or density
- comparing several random graphs that differ only in how their edges are placed

The full list of candidate pairs is built in memory, so time and memory grow as `n^2`
regardless of `m=`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes to add. The new nodes have no labels and get the next available
    node IDs.

<code>[m]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Exact number of edges to create among the new nodes. Must not exceed the number of
    possible pairs: `n(n - 1) / 2` for undirected graphs or `n(n - 1)` for directed ones.
    Larger values raise a `ValueError`.

<code>[directed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[bool]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Leave as `None` (default) to generate edges according to the graph's own
    directedness. Passing the graph's own setting (`graph.is_directed()`) does the same
    thing. Passing a value that differs from it generates the edges as if the graph had
    that directedness: `directed=True` on an undirected graph considers both orientations
    of each pair (so a pair can get two parallel edges), and `directed=False` on a
    directed graph considers each pair once and stores the edge in one direction.

<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Integer seed for the random number generator. The same `seed=` with the same `n=` and
    `m=` always picks the same edges. If `None` (default), the result differs on every
    call.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `m=` is larger than the number of possible edges among `n=` nodes.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_gnp_graph`) 

:   Join each pair with a fixed chance instead of a fixed edge count.

[](`~add_random_regular_graph`) 

:   Give every node the same degree as well.

[](`~configuration_model`) 

:   Wire a given degree sequence at random.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Unlike `add_gnp_graph()`, which includes each edge with some probability, this fixes the
number of edges and picks which pairs they join at random. `m=100` always gives exactly
`100` edges, whatever the seed:

::: {#aeaecfa7 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_gnm_graph(n=50, m=100, seed=23)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(50, 100)
```
:::
:::


In a directed graph the two orientations of a pair are separate candidates, so four nodes
can hold up to `4 * 3 = 12` edges. Asking for all of them gives a complete directed
graph:

::: {#473d3724 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
directed = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_gnm_graph(n=4, m=12)

directed.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
12
```
:::
:::


### Same count, different wiring

Changing `seed=` keeps the edge count but changes which pairs are joined. Both graphs
have `8` nodes and `10` edges, and so the same average degree, but the degrees are spread
differently. Seed `1` on the left and seed `5` on the right, with the nodes in the same
places:

::: {#89bc09f7 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
first = ec.Graph().add_gnm_graph(n=8, m=10, seed=1)
second = ec.Graph().add_gnm_graph(n=8, m=10, seed=5)

(
    first.get_node_df()["degree"].to_list(),
    second.get_node_df()["degree"].to_list(),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
([4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2], [3, 2, 2, 3, 3, 2, 2, 3])
```
:::
:::


